OK, so the cd is telling it what the current directory should be; and
the path statement is telling it where binaries you run are (so that
it can find patch).

"The system cannot find the file specified" is Windows' canned
translation for ENOENT when it doesn't know more context (the equivalent
on a typical Linux distribution is the well-known "No such file or
directory"). It doesn't say /which/ file, but I'm guessing it's
nh343-64bit.diff. Which directory is that file in? It should be
c:\nh343, and I'm guessing this is what's causing your problem.

The -p1 in the patch statement is an explanation of where your directory
tree is, relative to the directory tree in the patch; it means that the
difference between the current directory and the files you're patching,
is one away from the difference between the current directory when diff
was run and the files that were being diffed. This is something that you
have to know for your own setup and each individual patch, but 1 is
typically the most common value. You shouldn't need to change it if
you're following examples like that.

>
> Lastly the wiki says "Note that linux-style line endings will cause
> problems, you need to convert those into Windows-style ones!" and I
> have no idea what that means. or if it applies to me.

There are two common formats for text files; Windows style ends each
line with the bytes 0D 0A, UNIX-style just with 0A. To convert, just
find a text editor that knows both styles and use the Save As command
(you often get entries like "Text file (UNIX)" or "Text file (Windows)",
or a separate Encoding box which allows you to choose UNIX or Windows
encodings). Ignore "Mac encoding" even if you're sending to someone with
a Mac; that was an old system used by Mac OS Classic that nobody uses
nowadays (Mac OS X uses UNIX-style encoding).

